{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/9079-inversions-between-old-and-new","id":9079,"title":"inversions between old and new","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Demon: The Descent"],"posts":[{"date":"2013-11-12T13:07:54-08:00","id":9079,"title":"inversions between old and new","content":"I\u2019m going to be quite interested in the <b>Demon Translation Guide</b>. Reading through the text of <b>Demon: the Descent</b> that OPP graciously provided on the Kickstarter page, I found an interesting inversion between <b>Fallen</b> and <b>Descent</b>: in <b>Fallen</b>, Hell (i.e., the Abyss) is a place that you escaped, and are struggling to stay out of; in <b>Descent</b>, Hell is where you\u2019re trying to <i>go</i>: a place that\u2019s beyond the God-Machine\u2019s reach. In a way, it\u2019s similar to the inversion of Arcadia between <b>Changeling: the Dreaming</b> and <b>Changeling: the Lost</b>: in <b>Dreaming</b>, Arcadia was a place from which you were exiled, and to which you yearned to return; in <b>Lost</b>, Arcadia is a place that you escaped from, and which you are trying to avoid returning to.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-12T16:19:23-08:00","id":9271,"title":"","content":"Another inversion of a sort is that in Fallen, there is a great deal about heaven, prehistory, and the angelic state that the entities recall, and yet the fluff and crunch very obviously scream demon, creature of hell and torment, even when theyre trying to fight for redemption. Meanwhile, in Descent, while the majority of information is about the demonic condition and society, it gives the vibe far more of fallen angel, even when theyre fighting against God. i really like this twist, especially since due to sandbox approach, you can add as much or as little Demon to your Angel in both games. but still, i got the angel vibe very deliciously this time around.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/935-cleverest-of-things","name":"Cleverest of Things","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar935_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"650"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}